WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
We were about, I guess maybe a mile or half a mile probably from Conoco.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.560
And I remember Vista coming behind us.

3
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.260
They were to be an alcohol plant.

4
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:27.220
Mrs. Hazel [Verdine?] sold them some property, along with the Richardson and Campbell family. [Condea] Vista was right behind my home, period.

5
00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:31.680
Conoco when they came in . . . Conoco used to own Vista at one particular point in time.

6
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.940
And also Conoco was about . . . I would say about maybe a half a mile.

7
00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.520
But Vista was right in your backyard?

8
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:40.440
Right.

9
00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.840
How close would you say?

10
00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.780
If I had to judge the distance as a child,

11
00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:52.400
I would say maybe . . . Oh I’d say maybe about eighty to a hundred feet.

12
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:52.800
Okay.

13
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.060
And as a child, did you play out in the yard?

14
00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:55.820
Yes, I did.

15
00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.460
I played a lot out in our yard.

16
00:00:58.460 --> 00:00:59.860
That was my playground.

17
00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.480
I had nowhere to go.

18
00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.080
We couldn’t go places.

19
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:07.220
My dad was real strict with his children.

20
00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:14.700
If you wasn’t at school activities or something they gave you permission to go to like a dance or social, you had to be home.

21
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:15.980
You had to be in your place.

22
00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.387
They didn’t let us roam.

23
00:01:17.387 --> 00:01:24.280
With this petrochemical plant there, were you all aware that there was a danger to it or . . . ?

24
00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:29.780
We were never aware of the dangers of those chemical plants.

25
00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:30.760
We were never aware.

26
00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:35.980
We never really even knew what they had in them to be, you know, aware of the danger.

27
00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:45.960
But I remember grandma had a . . . Grandma [Lonnie?] had a swing on her front porch and the swing faced the east.

28
00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:51.300
And I would hear like this noise like something drawing ,umm umm,

29
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:58.260
and that swing would swing me and I would get so sleepy, and I would fall asleep on that swing.

30
00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:03.020
So if the chemicals were there causing harm, I didn’t know, I was a little child.

31
00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:07.540
I didn’t have no way of knowing the dangers and I don’t think my parents knew the danger.

32
00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:09.620
If they would have, we probably wouldn’t have lived there.

33
00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:10.760
How about the odor?

34
00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:12.480
Was there ever a strong odor?

35
00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.000
Oh, all the time.

36
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.500
Every day there was an odor in that area.

37
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.340
Every day.

38
00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:22.380
Some you could smell, some you couldn’t, but you knew it was there.

39
00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:33.840
But it was never any awareness came in the effect that people would cause other folks harm enough to make them die or kill them with these chemicals like,

40
00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.500
you know, getting cancer and stuff like that.

41
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:43.680
We just thought folk got old and died, or something misfortune happened where they just died.

42
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.960
So you were never aware that there could be an explosion and your life was in danger?

43
00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:54.860
We knew there of the explosions because they would explode, but we never once thought about us dying with it.
